In my never-ending quest of middle age, I've been [emulating Mac OS 9](/posts/20260810-newsheep-sheepshaver-mac-os-9-2-apple-silicon-jit/) and [getting Johnny Castaway running in a browser](/posts/20261019-johnny-castaway-browser/). Playing board games with my kids brings more of those childhood things back into view. I'll save the rant about what's happened to *The Game of Life* for another post.

Some of that nostalgia has found its way into the look of this site. I keep noticing the nineties in design again, often the grungier side: distressed type, rough textures, a certain deliberate mess. The examples I return to have a different kind of chaos. Japanese Sonic packaging, in particular, fits enormous lettering, bright colour, squiggles and zigzag edges around a character who already takes up most of the cover.

I wanted some of that personality here. The previous redesign wasn't long ago. It reflected the analytical side of my work, but I had made my personal site feel too clinical. It needed more of the things I enjoy looking at.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Japanese Sonic covers supplied the yellow disc, bold colour fields, compressed type and patterned edges.
> - A TurboGrafx-16 ad suggested a cut-paper treatment for selected images.
> - The homepage gets the expressive composition; the articles keep a clear reading column and upright text.

## From a datasheet to a cover

The earlier design treated the site as a technical document: graph paper, numbered sections, small labels, and rules around most things. That was a reasonable expression of work involving platforms and information architecture. On the homepage, though, the accumulation felt more like a register than an introduction.

I tried removing the frames. That helped, but left an awkward step where the headline started at the page edge and the biography jumped into another column. The alignment made sense in the stylesheet and looked awkward on the page.

The cartridge covers gave me a more useful starting point. One large statement could introduce the person, a featured project could show the work, and the writing list could serve as an index. My background in newspaper graphics and print production is part of this too. Large type, strong colour separation and a cover that leads into its contents are familiar territory.

