Japanese Mega Drive packaging, reproduced here as design references. Artwork and branding © SEGA. See the [VGDensetsu archive for the covers and its illustration and packaging credits](https://vgdensetsu.net/megadrivegallerys3/); individual design credits have not been independently verified here.

The yellow disc gives the headline a field to sit on. The black and cobalt lines have different weights of attention even though they share a typeface. A black ribbon carries the short description of the practice: “Platforms. Content. People. Connected by design.” The red diagonal makes room for the featured project as a tilted blue plate.

A small cluster of angular marks completes the composition. The same vocabulary appears selectively in the margins of code blocks and quotations: a serrated edge, a complete squiggle or a yellow accent. The marks have their own space, leaving the text clear.

There are visual connections here to [Sottsass’s *Casablanca* and *Carlton* drawings at the V&A](https://www.vam.ac.uk/info/collection-selection-boxes-postmodern-design) and [April Greiman’s work in *Eye on Design*](https://eyeondesign.aiga.org/april-greiman-is-still-ahead-of-the-curve/): shifts in scale, strong angles, lettering treated as an image. Those helped me think about the composition. The Sonic covers were the direct reference.

I drew new SVG marks for the site. The character illustrations and SEGA branding appear only in the reference images.

## A second reference: fragments on paper

I had a TurboGrafx-16 growing up and loved it, so its advertising belongs in this personal collection of references too. [This ad from *Electronic Gaming Monthly*](https://archives.tg-16.com/EGM_1989_07.htm) breaks a face and game imagery into unequal shards across a white field. Heavy black shapes hold it together, with small red and yellow overlaps. There is a lot happening, but also a surprising amount of empty paper.

