## Give the introduction something to point at

“Make it make sense” is the cover line. It needs some evidence beside it.

The featured plate links to [the UNDRR work connecting Storybook, structured content, design tokens, and Figma](/work/2026/impact-story-mangrove-figma/). Its title, image, and description come from the published entry. That puts evidence beside the introduction, where someone arriving for the first time can find it.

Below that, “Ideas in circulation” contains six recent entries from the live collection, with their real dates, teasers, and thumbnails. The masthead offers direct routes to writing, work, my profile, and search. The visual treatment is more assertive, but the navigation still has ordinary jobs to do.

## Let the article settle down

The [article layout](/posts/20261024-when-one-person-leaves-a-team-leaves/) carries over the near-white paper ground, yellow disc, and condensed title. Diagonal colour boundaries, small sawtooth edges and sparse geometric marks draw from the packaging’s print details; the paragraphs stay on plain paper. The reading column uses IBM Plex, upright paragraphs, and restrained cobalt headings. The contents are a native HTML disclosure, so readers can open the outline without another script.

The homepage headline uses [Science Gothic](https://github.com/googlefonts/science-gothic). Its letterforms already lean, so I kept the baseline level and left the rotation to the ribbon and project plate. [Anton](https://github.com/google/fonts/tree/main/ofl/anton) handles the other display headings, Plex Sans the paragraphs and controls, and [League Mono](https://github.com/theleagueof/league-mono) the code and small labels. The fonts are self-hosted; the homepage uses a small, fixed Science Gothic subset rather than the complete variable font.

The circle and diagonals are CSS shapes. SVG assets supply the margin marks and serrated edges. Most of the presentation works without JavaScript; the optional collage parallax is a small enhancement. The CSS toggle still removes the styling and leaves the content in document order.

## A site I recognise

The [design guide](/style-guide/design/) and [component reference](/style-guide/components/) carry these choices through the rest of the site. The [earlier proposals](/design-lab/megadrive/) remain available, and the implementation is in [PR #168](https://github.com/khawkins98/allaboutken-11ty/pull/168).

I still want the analytical structure: clear navigation, readable articles and work that explains itself. I also want a personal site to carry some of my taste and history. The games, the print graphics and the childhood computing projects are part of the same person who builds the platforms. Giving them some space here makes the site feel more like mine.

